## :landmark: The Bronx, a history {.title}

- The only borough on the mainland, in nineteen slides
- Tap a card to watch or read; every link opens Wikipedia

> Four centuries in a borough of 1.4 million: Lenape land, a Swedish farmer's name, the Grand Concourse, the stadium, the fires, the music, the comeback.

---

## :trees: Before the name

- The [Lenape](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lenape) called it Rananchqua and fished the river that would carry a Dutch settler's name
- [Jonas Bronck](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jonas_Bronck), from Sweden by way of Amsterdam, took 500 acres by the Harlem River in 1639
- "Bronck's land" became "the Bronx"; the river kept the name longest

> Bronck died in 1643. The farm passed on, the name did not: for two centuries the land was Westchester County, a scatter of villages and estates.

---

## :scroll: 1639: Bronck and the Lenape

![Bronck and the Lenape, 1639: a nineteenth-century painting of the treaty](https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1f/Signing_the_Treaty_with_the_Indians.jpg)

> A painting of the treaty, made long after; the only picture the story has. From Wikimedia Commons.

---

## :map: Becoming New York

- 1874: the towns west of the Bronx River joined New York City; 1895, the rest
- 1898: the five boroughs; 1914, [Bronx County](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Bronx), the last county made in the state
- The subway came in 1904, and with it the tenements and the apartment houses

> The next card asks Wikipedia for its summary of the whole history, for a reader who wants the long version.

---

## :book-open: The long version

https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_the_Bronx

