## :road: The Grand Concourse

- Laid out by [Louis Aloys Risse](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Louis_Aloys_Risse), a French engineer, on the Champs-Élysées's plan; opened 1909
- Four miles of boulevard, and along it the finest run of [Art Deco](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grand_Concourse_(Bronx)) apartment houses in the country
- The Bronx of the 1920s and 30s: a step up from the Lower East Side

> Jewish, Irish and Italian families came up the Concourse in the decades the subway made it possible; the borough doubled and doubled again.

---

## :trophy: 1923: the House that Ruth Built

- [Yankee Stadium](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yankee_Stadium_(1923)) opened across the river from the Polo Grounds, the first three-tiered park in baseball
- Ruth hit a home run on opening day; the Yankees won their first title that October

---

## :flag: Opening day, 18 April 1923

![Opening Day at Yankee Stadium, 18 April 1923: the flag going up](https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/89/Opening_Day_1923_Yankee_Stadium_%28baseball%29_LCCN2014715917.jpg)

> The Library of Congress's, from the Bain collection.

---

## :construction: The Cross Bronx Expressway

- [Robert Moses](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Moses) cut the [expressway](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cross_Bronx_Expressway) through East Tremont from 1948 to 1972
- Sixty thousand people moved; the neighbourhoods north and south of the trench never knit back

> Marshall Berman, who grew up there, wrote the chapter of All That Is Solid Melts into Air that made the road a byword.

---

